WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:04.835
Can language models carry out causal analysis reliably and can post-training

00:00:04.845 --> 00:00:08.625
improve open models without damaging other skills

00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:13.907
This project brings together Causal Bench and a study of causal post-training

00:00:14.478 --> 00:00:19.365
We first measure what models can do on causal inference and experimentation

00:00:19.375 --> 00:00:24.494
tasks We then study what improves and what breaks when an open model

00:00:24.504 --> 00:00:25.938
is trained for this domain

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.072
We begin with Causal Bench using well-specified questions whose answers

00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:33.573
can be checked against known ground truth

00:00:34.603 --> 00:00:39.594
We constructed 328 questions covering 16 topics

00:00:40.186 --> 00:00:42.523
These questions come from three sources

00:00:43.129 --> 00:00:47.887
First our own data-generating processes where we explicitly define

00:00:47.897 --> 00:00:50.955
the causal structure and know the underlying truth

00:00:51.561 --> 00:00:55.483
Second questions adapted from real research datasets

00:00:56.061 --> 00:00:59.867
And third questions taken from public causal benchmarks

00:01:00.436 --> 00:01:04.988
During testing we give the model a clear task description including

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:08.675
the meaning of each column and the first five rows of the data

00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:13.719
The data is clean and the relevant assumptions are specified or follow

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:15.227
a standard textbook setting

00:01:15.824 --> 00:01:19.745
However the model may still need to select the appropriate causal

00:01:19.755 --> 00:01:20.699
inference method

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:25.706
Importantly the model cannot access the full dataset or execute its

00:01:25.716 --> 00:01:26.428
own code

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.928
Instead it produces an analysis script and a structured answer

00:01:31.478 --> 00:01:36.468
We then execute the script on hidden data and evaluate it using programmatic

00:01:36.478 --> 00:01:41.907
checks including identification method selection estimates intervals

00:01:41.994 --> 00:01:43.119
and diagnostics

00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:47.581
This helps us evaluate whether models can implement correct causal

00:01:47.591 --> 00:01:50.714
analysis rather than simply guessing an answer

00:01:51.757 --> 00:01:56.580
Models generally perform very well on textbook causal concepts

00:01:57.238 --> 00:02:01.932
For example their average performance on causal graphs and identifiability

00:02:01.942 --> 00:02:05.979
questions is around 97 to 98 percent

00:02:06.574 --> 00:02:11.848
However experiment design and diagnosis are more difficult with average

00:02:11.858 --> 00:02:15.212
pass rates of 74 and 77 percent

00:02:16.228 --> 00:02:18.268
Here are the results from eleven models

00:02:18.853 --> 00:02:23.071
We find that the strongest frontier models perform remarkably well

00:02:23.645 --> 00:02:29.511
The top five models achieve approximately 93 to 95 percent pass rates

00:02:29.521 --> 00:02:33.557
on well-specified tasks and their differences are not statistically

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:34.512
distinguishable

00:02:35.103 --> 00:02:37.414
But several interesting patterns emerge

00:02:38.058 --> 00:02:42.533
First weaker models often fail because of engineering issues rather

00:02:42.543 --> 00:02:45.279
than purely conceptual misunderstandings

00:02:45.853 --> 00:02:50.121
Around 40 percent of their failures involve crashing scripts truncated

00:02:50.131 --> 00:02:52.455
outputs or incorrect formatting

00:02:53.007 --> 00:02:57.726
Second coding requirements create a larger gap between strong and

00:02:57.736 --> 00:02:58.784
weak models

00:02:59.327 --> 00:03:04.499
On tasks requiring scripts the top five models achieve around 90 percent

00:03:04.662 --> 00:03:07.385
compared with 65 percent for the others

00:03:07.978 --> 00:03:12.569
So our main takeaway is that knowing causal concepts is no longer

00:03:12.579 --> 00:03:16.718
the main challenge for frontier models Applying those concepts to

00:03:16.728 --> 00:03:18.742
actual analysis is harder

00:03:19.353 --> 00:03:23.312
And this motivates our next version of Causal Bench where models have

00:03:23.322 --> 00:03:28.016
greater autonomy but must deal with more realistic problems including

00:03:28.026 --> 00:03:32.965
noisy data data-quality issues and potential violations of assumptions

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:38.262
The benchmark measures current capabilities The next question is whether

00:03:38.272 --> 00:03:40.635
we can improve them through post-training

00:03:41.215 --> 00:03:45.922
Many companies need open models that can run locally on sensitive

00:03:45.932 --> 00:03:46.876
internal data

00:03:47.465 --> 00:03:51.605
We study whether training an open model for causal analysis improves

00:03:51.615 --> 00:03:55.677
its target performance while preserving tool use and performance on

00:03:55.687 --> 00:03:56.580
other tests

00:03:57.603 --> 00:04:03.933
We start with Qwen3 . 8-27B and use three training stages continued

00:04:03.943 --> 00:04:07.607
pre-training supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning

00:04:08.225 --> 00:04:14.041
For continued pre-training we use text from DoWhy and EconML documentation

00:04:14.616 --> 00:04:20.663
However the initial corpus contains only around 77,000 tokens and

00:04:20.673 --> 00:04:23.989
we observe almost no downstream improvement

00:04:24.549 --> 00:04:30.673
Next we perform supervised fine-tuning using approximately 13,800

00:04:30.683 --> 00:04:34.862
question-answer examples covering causal graphs full analysis cases

00:04:34.948 --> 00:04:35.983
and tool use

00:04:36.549 --> 00:04:42.132
Our in-domain performance improves substantially from around 0.59

00:04:42.257 --> 00:04:43.730
to 0.87

00:04:44.311 --> 00:04:46.635
But the external results are much worse

00:04:47.228 --> 00:04:52.128
Performance on CLadder drops by 0.30 and tool-calling performance

00:04:52.138 --> 00:04:54.333
drops by 0.21

00:04:55.353 --> 00:04:57.612
We identify two problems

00:04:58.225 --> 00:05:03.550
First the model learns our training-data format too strongly reducing

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.502
its ability to follow different output formats

00:05:07.061 --> 00:05:12.013
Second every tool-calling example in our training data contains exactly

00:05:12.023 --> 00:05:13.187
one tool call

00:05:13.757 --> 00:05:18.515
As a result the model learns a very narrow pattern It struggles when

00:05:18.525 --> 00:05:22.704
a task requires no tool calls or multiple tool calls

00:05:23.337 --> 00:05:28.482
Finally we try reinforcement learning using GRPO but observe almost

00:05:28.492 --> 00:05:30.261
no additional improvement

00:05:30.811 --> 00:05:35.067
A possible explanation is that the combination of LoRA a small learning

00:05:35.077 --> 00:05:39.488
rate and KL regularization restricts how much the model can change

00:05:40.507 --> 00:05:44.440
To address these problems we conduct a second independent training

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.258
run which we call Line B

00:05:46.853 --> 00:05:51.432
We first expand the continued pre-training corpus to approximately

00:05:51.518 --> 00:05:54.666
1.57 million tokens

00:05:55.228 --> 00:06:00.154
During supervised fine-tuning we also introduce replay data from general-purpose

00:06:00.164 --> 00:06:03.544
tasks to help preserve existing capabilities

00:06:04.116 --> 00:06:07.032
However tool-calling performance still drops

00:06:08.061 --> 00:06:12.910
We then identify that our replay data contains too few examples of

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:14.998
the missing tool-use patterns

00:06:15.561 --> 00:06:21.337
So we create approximately 1,600 targeted counterexamples including

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:25.926
cases requiring no tool calls and multiple parallel tool calls

00:06:26.507 --> 00:06:31.472
After two targeted patches tool-calling performance recovers substantially

00:06:31.752 --> 00:06:36.163
while our in-domain causal performance remains almost unchanged

00:06:36.715 --> 00:06:39.090
We also observe some recovery on CLadder

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:44.784
Together benchmarking and post-training show why a single score is

00:06:44.794 --> 00:06:45.674
not enough

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:50.834
Causal Bench tests whether models can turn causal knowledge into working

00:06:50.844 --> 00:06:55.628
analysis Our training runs show that higher in-domain accuracy can

00:06:55.638 --> 00:06:57.807
come with losses on other tasks

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:02.829
Targeted training examples recover much of the lost tool performance

00:07:02.902 --> 00:07:07.196
while preserving the in-domain gain This does not establish broad

00:07:07.206 --> 00:07:11.629
improvement across causal benchmarks and reinforcement learning adds

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:14.530
no measurable in-domain gain in these runs

00:07:15.103 --> 00:07:19.101
The goal is to improve causal capabilities while checking what the

00:07:19.111 --> 00:07:23.123
model preserves not just what it learns on the training distribution
